  index.html   — <h1>Tasks</h1> and an empty <main>

  Single commit, [38;5;153minitial: empty tasks page[39m. There is no task model, no users, no persistence, no server, no build
  tooling. "Notify users when tasks they care about change" presupposes all four of those: something to [3mbe[0m a task,
  someone to [3mbe[0m a user, a thing that detects a [3mchange[0m, and a channel to [3mreach[0m them. Right now none of it exists, so a
  notifications spec written against this repo would mostly be a spec for the app underneath it.

  That's a decomposition problem, not a detail to refine — so it's the first thing to settle.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether I'm designing notifications [3mfor an app that exists somewhere I haven't seen[0m, or designing it [3magainst
[0m  [3man app that also has to be built[0m, or [3mdeferring[0m it until the task app exists.

  - [1mThe real app is elsewhere[0m — you point me at the actual codebase (or describe its stack, data model, and auth) and I
    design notifications to fit it. Buys: a spec grounded in real constraints. Costs: nothing, if the code exists. Rules
    out: nothing.
  - [1mBuild tasks first, notifications second[0m — we brainstorm the task app as its own spec, then notifications as a
    follow-on. Buys: each subsystem gets a real design instead of a guess. Costs: notifications is not what we work on
    today. Rules out: nothing — this is the ordering the decomposition implies.
  - [1mDesign notifications now against an assumed app[0m — I write the spec on stated assumptions about the task model and
    auth. Buys: the notifications design exists today. Costs: every assumption is a place the spec can be wrong, and the
    data-model choices are expensive to redo. Rules out: little, but risks rework.
  - [1mScope to a self-contained client-side demo[0m — tasks and notifications both in-browser, no server, no real users.
    Buys: something running fast. Costs: "users" becomes fictional; multi-user notification delivery is exactly the part
    that doesn't survive the jump to a real backend. Rules out: reusing most of the delivery design later.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first, if the real app exists.[0m Notifications is defined almost entirely by what it hooks into —
  the change events, the identity model, whether there's a server to push from. Designing it blind means guessing the
  load-bearing parts. If this repo genuinely is the whole app, then the second: tasks first, notifications as its own
  cycle after.

